{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/301470-freeholds-at-war","id":301470,"title":"Freeholds at War","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-11-03T13:23:45-08:00","id":301470,"title":"Freeholds at War","content":"It's pretty self explanatory. I'm working at a homebrew setting in lovely Reno Nevada, and I thought I would make the plot about a war between two Freeholds. A sort of Cold War, escalating into sabotage, perhaps escalating into full out fights. I'm just a little unsure how to handle it. I don't know if anyone else has fiddled with this idea, but if anyone has, I'd love to hear about it. <br /> <br /> -rick out","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3309-electrick","name":"electrick","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3309_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-03T18:00:35-08:00","id":301628,"title":"","content":"There's a ton of useful stuff in Swords at Dawn about freeholds at war (against one another or otherwise).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1168-hobbitguy1420","name":"HobbitGuy1420","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"332"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-08T05:50:40-08:00","id":306607,"title":"","content":"Well, most Freehold wars are going to involve a lot of guerilla and criminal tactics from ambushing some guy in an alleyway with metal pipes to firebombing buildings. The Hedge provides a place for more full scale battles since the cops aren't going to show up. You can have changelings charging in full plate mail wielding swords and fire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3497-urbicamortis","name":"UrbicaMortis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-10T08:26:32-08:00","id":308230,"title":"","content":"Oh man, I ran a game set in Las Vegas a few years back that led to the PC's founding their own freehold, and things escalating to full blown war with the old freehold in Vegas.<br /> <br /> In their case what escalated tensions was recruitment, since they would regularly poach members of the old freehold to join them.<br /> <br /> A few things that highlighted the war.<br /> <br /> 1) Violence: Goblin Fruit offers easy access to healing, so both sides were pretty willing to toss around lethal force since it's fairly difficult to accidentally murder someone if they've got their motley around. When one side actually killed another though things ramped up really fast, and it's hard to gauge whether someone was trying to really kill you and you narrowly avoided death, or they were just trying to hurt you bad.<br /> <br /> 2) Clarity: On several occasions people would be drugged as an attack on their Clarity. Lacing bottled water with LSD, slowly replacing anti-psychotics someone was taking with other drugs, that sort of thing. <br /> <br /> 3) Onieromancy: Looking to gain intelligence, work on flipping an enemy agent, or just plague them with nightmares? Onieromancy is your friend, and your onieromancers are likely to be like your special agents.<br /> <br /> 4) Proxy Fights: The biggest things that happened in my game involved fighting by proxy. With the ability to magic up social merits using pledges both sides would use mortal agencies to attack one another. Calling in mortal hit-men, getting the IRS to audit someone, etc... <br /> <br /> So for a cold war type setup, you'll want to consider the ground rules for the conflict, which will be influenced by resource availability. If goblin fruit is plentiful, then casual violence is probably acceptable. If you don't want to deal with onieromancy dickery you might just assume that a treaty was signed banning it's use in intra-freehold conflict outside of duels and things. It could get paranoid if it stays on the table since you can do things like rewrite memories with onieromancy. Proxy fights will be a big deal, much like they were in the cold war, and will be very difficult to defend against in advance, you just have to react, or plan your own counter-move.<br /> <br /> From the outside a lot of this will look like a criminal turf war, and might offer you a cool thematic way to style it. Work out some colors and things people use to symbolism their membership in a particular freehold, and provides a nice non-supernatural cover for the activities of the freeholds.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4270-literatzi","name":"literatzi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4270_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}